{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1778869304,"nonce":"gZRDKBxQjpWh42kx47tq5YrNIdg=","message":"Status: draft design spec  \nRelated issues: Feedbax `e33f487`, `f68cf66`, `8d94424`, `93f79f2`; Mandible `2472c51`, `0516fc0`\n\n\nFeedbax needs durable records for experiment lineage and a stable boundary for use by\nMandible, agents, project packages, scripts, and Studio. This spec combines two tightly\ncoupled design threads:\n\n- Manifest-first run lineage, with databases as rebuildable indexes.\n- A Feedbax provider contract for specs, components, validation, execution, artifacts,\n  and Studio entry points.\n\nThe intent is architectural: define the shape of the system before implementation work\nfragments into one-off scripts, hidden Studio state, or Mandible-specific hardcoding.\n\n\n1. Manifests are canonical. Databases index manifests; they are not the only source of\n   truth.\n2. Large outputs are artifacts. Manifests reference them by stable IDs, hashes, and\n   roles rather than assuming fragile absolute paths.\n3. Feedbax owns domain semantics. Mandible may orchestrate and remember, but Feedbax\n   validates Feedbax specs and runs Feedbax computations.\n4. Agents need bounded contracts. They should discover schemas, valid components, and\n   execution capabilities instead of guessing internal Python APIs.\n5. Registry cleanup should generalize current behavior, not require a disruptive rewrite\n   of the graph/component model.\n6. Human review remains part of the path for new executable code, especially\n   agent-created leaf components.\n\n\nThese are intentionally prescriptive because weakening them would undermine the whole\narchitecture:\n\n- Executed runs must produce durable manifests, not only database rows or UI state.\n- Large artifacts must be referenced with stable metadata and content hashes wherever\n  possible.\n- The database must be rebuildable from manifests and artifact metadata.\n- Feedbax must validate Feedbax specs and component references before execution.\n- Durable graph specs must reference component type IDs that can be resolved by a\n  Feedbax-owned registry/contract.\n- Durable task and loss specs must reference type IDs that can be resolved by a\n  Feedbax-owned registry/contract.\n- Task, biomechanics, and loss contracts must allow structured/visual authoring in\n  Studio, not only selection among pre-programmed Python presets.\n- Durable analysis specs must reference analysis type IDs that can be resolved by a\n  Feedbax-owned registry/contract.\n- Leaf components used in durable runs must resolve to reviewed/importable Python code,\n  or the manifest must explicitly mark the run as depending on unreviewed local code.\n- Project-defined tasks, losses, and training/evaluation protocols used in durable runs\n  must resolve to reviewed/importable Python code, or the manifest must explicitly mark\n  the run as depending on unreviewed local code.\n- Analyses used in durable manifests must resolve to reviewed/importable Python code, or\n  the manifest must explicitly mark the result as depending on unreviewed local code.\n- Mandible integration must go through provider capabilities and schemas rather than\n  hardcoded knowledge of Feedbax internals.\n\nOther details in this document are design guidance unless they are needed to satisfy the\nrequirements above. Implementation agents should inspect the current code closely and may\nchoose a better local factoring, but they should call out any deviation from these\nrequirements.\n\n\n- This is not a full Mandible design spec.\n- This does not require replacing Feedbax's current `Component`/`Graph` execution model.\n- This does not require moving built-in components out of normal Feedbax source files.\n- This does not prescribe one transport immediately; HTTP, CLI, Python entry points, and\n  MCP-style tools can all be staged.\n- This does not specify Studio UX in detail, except where Studio depends on manifest or\n  provider semantics.\n\n\nFeedbax already has pieces of the desired system:\n\n- `feedbax.graph.Component`, `Graph`, and `Wire` define the execution model.\n- `feedbax.web.models.graph.GraphSpec` and related models represent Studio graph specs.\n- `feedbax.web.services.component_registry.ComponentRegistry` exposes Studio-visible\n  component metadata.\n- `feedbax.web.serialization.spec_to_graph()` instantiates graph specs, but currently\n  does so through a large hardcoded type-dispatch chain.\n- `feedbax.plugins.registry.ExperimentRegistry` discovers project packages for training,\n  analysis, config, and figure routing.\n- Existing run/evaluation/database code gives Feedbax a partial indexing layer.\n\nThe gap is that these pieces do not yet form one durable contract. A run may depend on\nhidden code paths, local files, UI state, hardcoded deserialization, or artifacts whose\nmeaning is not captured in a portable manifest.\n\n\n**Spec**: A declarative request or configuration before execution. Examples:\n`GraphSpec`, `TrainingRunSpec`, `EvaluationRunSpec`, `AnalysisRunSpec`,\n`ReportSpec`.\n\n**Manifest**: A durable record emitted after creation or execution. Examples:\n`TrainingRunManifest`, `EvaluationRunManifest`, `AnalysisRunManifest`,\n`ReportManifest`.\n\n**Artifact**: A large or non-source output such as a checkpoint, history array,\ntrajectory dataset, rendered figure, table, log, or report bundle.\n\n**Artifact reference**: A manifest entry that identifies an artifact by role, media\ntype, hash, storage backend, and optional orchestrator-provided ID.\n\n**Provider**: Feedbax acting as a domain tool that declares what it can validate, open,\nrun, import, export, and publish.\n\n**Component registry**: The Feedbax-owned catalog of component definitions, builders,\nmetadata, schemas, compatibility constraints, and provenance.\n\n\nFeedbax should represent experiment lineage through portable manifests. A database is\nstill valuable for query and UI performance, but it should be rebuildable from manifests\nand artifact metadata.\n\n\nThe initial manifest family should cover:\n\n- `ProjectManifest`: optional project-level context, package identity, config roots,\n  provider version, and project-specific extension packages.\n- `GraphSpecManifest`: a versioned graph/model/task/loss spec and its component\n  references.\n- `TrainingRunSetManifest`: a named collection of related training runs, usually a\n  sweep, replicate group, or matrix cell.\n- `TrainingRunManifest`: one training execution, including input specs, overrides,\n  code/provenance, status, metrics, and artifacts.\n- `EvaluationRunManifest`: one evaluation execution against one or more trained models.\n- `AnalysisRunManifest`: one deterministic analysis execution over run/eval artifacts.\n- `ReportManifest`: one report product, including deterministic figures/tables and,\n  optionally, narrative text with author/provenance metadata.\n\nThese do not all need to be implemented at once. The important first step is choosing\nnames, IDs, lineage edges, and artifact-reference conventions that can survive import,\nexport, and database rebuilds.\n\n\nEvery manifest should have a stable identity and schema version:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"kind\": \"TrainingRunManifest\",\n  \"schema_version\": \"feedbax.manifest.v1\",\n  \"id\": \"feedbax-training-run:\u003cuuid-or-content-id\u003e\",\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-05-15T00:00:00-04:00\",\n  \"feedbax_version\": \"0.1.2\",\n  \"provider_version\": \"feedbax-provider.v1\"\n}\n```\n\nIDs should be stable enough for cross-references. They do not need to be purely\ncontent-addressed, because a run manifest may be created before all artifacts exist, but\nthe manifest should include content hashes where possible.\n\n\nEach execution manifest should capture:\n\n- Source repository URL or local project identity.\n- Source commit, dirty status, and optionally a patch/artifact reference.\n- Python package versions or an environment lockfile artifact.\n- Entrypoint used, such as CLI command, Studio action, worker endpoint, or analysis\n  module key.\n- Issue IDs, work-unit IDs, Mandible session IDs, or human notes when available.\n- Parent specs/manifests/artifacts.\n\nExample:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"provenance\": {\n    \"source_commit\": \"abc1234\",\n    \"dirty\": false,\n    \"entrypoint\": {\n      \"kind\": \"feedbax-cli\",\n      \"command\": \"feedbax-train rlrmp/part2_5\"\n    },\n    \"issues\": [\"rlrmp:35f64be\"],\n    \"parents\": [\n      {\"kind\": \"GraphSpecManifest\", \"id\": \"feedbax-graph-spec:...\"}\n    ]\n  }\n}\n```\n\n\nManifests should distinguish base specs from run-specific overrides:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"graph_spec\": {\"ref\": \"manifest://feedbax-graph-spec/pm-reaching-v2\"},\n  \"training_spec\": {\n    \"optimizer\": {\"type\": \"adamw\", \"learning_rate\": 0.001},\n    \"n_batches\": 2000,\n    \"batch_size\": 256\n  },\n  \"overrides\": [\n    {\"path\": \"/task/target_radius\", \"value\": 0.06},\n    {\"path\": \"/loss/terms/activity_l2/weight\", \"value\": 0.001}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe exact path language can be JSON Pointer, dotted paths, or a typed patch format. The\nkey requirement is that overrides are machine-readable and do not require interpreting a\nnotebook or free-text issue comment.\n\n\nLarge outputs should not be embedded in manifests. A manifest should reference artifacts\nby role and stable identity:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"artifacts\": [\n    {\n      \"role\": \"training_checkpoint\",\n      \"logical_name\": \"model.eqx\",\n      \"artifact_id\": \"artifact://sha256/...\",\n      \"sha256\": \"...\",\n      \"media_type\": \"application/x-equinox\",\n      \"size_bytes\": 123456789,\n      \"storage_backend\": \"mandible-local\",\n      \"uri\": null,\n      \"metadata\": {\n        \"batch\": 2000\n      }\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nWhen Mandible is present, it may supply `artifact_id` and storage metadata. When Mandible\nis absent, Feedbax should still be able to emit local artifact references with paths and\nhashes, and Mandible can ingest them later.\n\n\nFeedbax should support a portable on-disk layout. A first pass could be:\n\n```text\nfeedbax_runs/\n  manifests/\n    graph_specs/\n    training_run_sets/\n    training_runs/\n    evaluation_runs/\n    analysis_runs/\n    reports/\n  artifacts/\n    sha256/\n  index/\n    feedbax.sqlite\n```\n\nThe exact default path can be project-config driven. The important rule is that the\ndatabase can be removed and rebuilt from `manifests/` plus artifact metadata.\n\n\nThe database should index queryable fields from manifests:\n\n- Manifest kind, ID, schema version, timestamps, and status.\n- Project/package, source commit, and entrypoint.\n- Run-set membership and lineage edges.\n- Flattened hyperparameters and typed override paths.\n- Artifact IDs, roles, media types, hashes, and storage backends.\n- Summary metrics such as final loss, success rate, or evaluation score.\n- Compatibility metadata, including schema/provider versions.\n\nThe database should not become the only place where lineage exists. If a field is needed\nto reconstruct, review, import, or publish a run, it belongs in a manifest first.\n\n\nFeedbax already has useful hash-based records:\n\n- `ModelRecord`: model/checkpoint identity, training hyperparameters, version info,\n  train history and replicate-info paths.\n- `EvaluationRecord`: evaluated model hashes, evaluation parameters, perturbation/task\n  variant/SISU/setup metadata, and version info.\n- `FigureRecord`: figure identity, evaluation linkage, saved formats, model hashes, and\n  figure path.\n- Figure reproducibility specs from `save_figure_with_spec`, including input paths,\n  SHA-256 digests, transform metadata, plot kwargs, versions, seed, and timestamp.\n\nThese should be treated as migration/index sources, not as the final manifest model.\nAn implementation should be able to reconstruct initial manifests from existing database\nrecords and files where possible, but it should also mark uncertainty when old records do\nnot contain enough information to reproduce a run or analysis faithfully.\n\nThe manifest layer should preserve the useful existing behavior: content hashes,\nversion info, project package routing, and figure specs. It should add the missing\nportable lineage: explicit manifest kinds, parent/child edges, artifact roles, stable\nregistry type IDs, provider/schema versions, and review/provenance metadata.\n\n\n---\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}